= Fourier series
{c}
{wiki}
{title2=$f(x)\sim a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}[a_n\cos(nx)+b_n\sin(nx)]$}

= Fourier expansion
{c}
{synonym}

A <Fourier series> describes a <periodic function> through <sines> and <cosines> whose frequencies are <integer> multiples of one base <frequency>. These <Fourier harmonics> give a useful alternative to describing the value at every input: each <Fourier coefficient> measures how much of one oscillating pattern is present. For a real, integrable <function> of period $2\pi$, the real form is
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\bigl[a_n\cos(nx)+b_n\sin(nx)\bigr],
\qquad
a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\cos(nx)\,dx,
\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\sin(nx)\,dx.
$$
The cosine formula includes $n=0$; the sine formula uses $n\geq1$. Thus $a_0/2$ is the mean value over a <period>, not $a_0$ itself. The symbol $\sim$ associates a <function> with its <Fourier coefficients>; an equality requires a specified kind of convergence, such as <pointwise convergence>. For general period $T>0$, replace $nx$ by $2\pi nt/T$ and the coefficient prefactor by $2/T$. <Fourier orthogonality> explains why integrating against one mode removes the others. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-18-015-topics-in-fourier-analysis-spring-2024/mitres_18_015_s24_lec01.pdf[Daniel Stroock, Basic Theory of Fourier Series: orthogonality and periodic expansions]{ref}.

For a finite example, take
$$
f(x)=\frac12+\cos x+\frac12\sin(2x).
$$
Its mean is $1/2$, its real <Fourier coefficients> are $a_0=1$, $a_1=1$, $b_2=1/2$, and all other $a_n,b_n$ vanish. At $x=\pi/4$, the three contributions add to $1/2+1/\sqrt2+1/2=1+1/\sqrt2\approx1.70710678$. The <function> is already a <trigonometric polynomial>, so retaining these modes reconstructs it exactly. Here $x$ is an angular coordinate in <radians>; if it instead represents time, a base <angular frequency> must supply the corresponding units.

\Image[fourier-harmonic-reconstruction.png]
{title=Three simple components reconstruct a <periodic> <signal> exactly}
{description=The <constant function> $1/2$, the <cosine> $\cos x$, and the <sine> $\tfrac12\sin(2x)$ add to the displayed <trigonometric polynomial>. The marked input $x=\pi/4$ has output $1+1/\sqrt2$. Each contribution can be measured separately by its <Fourier coefficient>.}

The change of viewpoint is useful because many <linear> problems act separately on these modes. The second <derivative> maps $\sin(nx)$ to $-n^2\sin(nx)$ and maps $\cos(nx)$ in the same way. Consequently a differential equation that is complicated for a whole profile can become a simpler calculation for each <Fourier harmonic>. A <linear time-invariant system> similarly has a separate response to each <complex exponential>. <Superposition> combines the responses when the relevant sums and operations are justified. https://ocw.mit.edu/ans7870/18/18.013a/textbook/HTML/chapter30/section08.html[MIT Calculus with Applications, section 30.8: Fourier series and forced responses]{ref}.

The infinite case contains a subtlety absent from the finite example. Under <pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series>, the value at a <jump discontinuity> is the average of the two one-sided limits. For a <square-integrable function>, the <complex Fourier series> instead converges in the $L^2$ norm: the integrated squared error tends to zero. Neither statement says that the error becomes small everywhere at once. A <square wave> shows the difference vividly through the <Gibbs phenomenon>.

A historical source for this connection between profiles and modes is <Joseph Fourier>'s 1822 book Théorie analytique de la chaleur. Chapter III develops expansions in <sines> and <cosines> while studying the propagation of <heat>. The <heat equation> gives a continuing application of this approach. <Théorie analytique de la chaleur by Joseph Fourier/Propagation de la chaleur dans un solide rectangle infini>{ref}.

= Fourier series of a periodic rectangular pulse
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$a_n=(2/T)\operatorname{sinc}(\pi n w/T)$}

An even train of <rectangular pulses> of width $w<T$, <period> $T$ and unit area per pulse has <Fourier coefficients> $a_n=(2/T)\operatorname{sinc}(\pi nw/T)$ for $n\geq0$. Here the <sinc function> is normalized as $\operatorname{sinc}z=\sin z/z$, and the constant contribution is $a_0/2=1/T$. Integrating the <cosine> over the pulse proves the formula; evenness eliminates the <sine> coefficients. At a pulse edge, <pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series> gives the mean of the one-sided values rather than a prescribed endpoint value.

= Pulse-width scale of Fourier coefficients
{parent=Fourier series of a periodic rectangular pulse}
{title2=$n\sim T/w$}

For the <Fourier series of a periodic rectangular pulse>, the <sinc function> is nearly one when $nw/T\ll1$ and has its first zero at $nw/T=1$. Thus narrowing a pulse increases the range of significant <Fourier coefficients>. Only integer harmonic indices occur, so an envelope zero need not be an exactly vanishing coefficient.

= Fourier series of a half-wave rectified ramp
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$f(x)=x+|x|\quad(-1<x<1)$}

Extend $f(x)=\max(0,2x)$ on $(-1,1)$ with period two. Its <Fourier coefficients> in the basis $\cos(n\pi x),\sin(n\pi x)$ are $a_0=1$, $a_n=2[(-1)^n-1]/(n\pi)^2$, and $b_n=2(-1)^{n+1}/(n\pi)$, by <integration by parts> on $(0,1)$. Thus
$$
f(x)\sim\frac12-\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{r=0}^{\infty}\frac{\cos((2r+1)\pi x)}{(2r+1)^2}+\frac2\pi\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\sin(n\pi x)}n.
$$
Its <even> part is the periodic extension of $|x|$ and its <odd> part is the <Fourier series of a periodic sawtooth> for $x$. The cosine terms converge absolutely and uniformly; the sine terms account for the jump at odd integers. At each jump, the full <Fourier series> has value $1$, the average of the two one-sided limits $0$ and $2$.

= Fourier series of a noninteger-frequency cosine
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$\cos(\mu x),\quad\mu\in\mathbb R\setminus\mathbb Z$}

Extend $\cos(\mu x)$ from $[-\pi,\pi]$ with period $2\pi$. It is an <even function>, so all sine <Fourier coefficients> vanish. The product-to-sum identity gives
$$
a_0=\frac{2\sin(\pi\mu)}{\pi\mu},\qquad
a_n=\frac{2\mu(-1)^n\sin(\pi\mu)}{\pi(\mu^2-n^2)}\quad(n\geq1).
$$
Indeed, integrate $\cos(\mu x)\cos(nx)$ on $[0,\pi]$ and use $\sin(\pi(\mu\pm n))=(-1)^n\sin(\pi\mu)$. Thus
$$
f(x)=\frac{\sin(\pi\mu)}{\pi\mu}
+\frac{2\mu\sin(\pi\mu)}\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n\cos(nx)}{\mu^2-n^2}.
$$
The coefficients are $O(n^{-2})$, so the series converges absolutely and uniformly. The <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> identifies it with the periodic function everywhere, including the endpoints because the two endpoint values agree. Substituting a noninteger rational frequency and a convenient $x$ yields explicit <series> sums. Integer frequencies require taking removable limits rather than substituting into a zero denominator.

= Fourier solution of a first-order equation with periodic forcing
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$\alpha y'+\beta y=f$}

Let $\alpha,\beta$ be nonzero real constants and let $f$ be a <smooth> <periodic function> of period $2\pi$, with real <Fourier coefficients> $c_n,d_n$. Write the unknown <periodic solution> with <Fourier coefficients> $a_n,b_n$. <Fourier orthogonality> applied to the equation gives
$$
\beta a_0=c_0,\qquad
\beta a_n+\alpha n b_n=c_n,\qquad
\beta b_n-\alpha n a_n=d_n.
$$
Solving these two-by-two <linear systems> gives
$$
a_n=\frac{\beta c_n-\alpha n d_n}{\beta^2+\alpha^2n^2},\qquad
b_n=\frac{\alpha n c_n+\beta d_n}{\beta^2+\alpha^2n^2}\quad(n\geq1).
$$
The smooth forcing has rapidly decreasing <Fourier coefficients>, so the resulting series and its <derivatives> converge and satisfy the equation. Uniqueness follows because the difference of two solutions is $Ce^{-\beta x/\alpha}$, which is periodic only when $C=0$. A single sinusoidal forcing therefore gives a single sinusoidal response with changed amplitude and phase.

= Fourier series of a periodically extended exponential
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$f(x)=e^{\kappa x},\quad -L<x<L$}
{c}

Let $L>0$ and real $\kappa\ne0$. Extend $e^{\kappa x}$ with period $2L$. In the <Fourier series> convention $a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}[a_n\cos(n\pi x/L)+b_n\sin(n\pi x/L)]$, put $q_n=n\pi/L$. Direct <integration> gives
$$
a_0=\frac{2\sinh(\kappa L)}{\kappa L},\qquad
a_n=\frac{2\kappa(-1)^n\sinh(\kappa L)}{L(\kappa^2+q_n^2)},\qquad
b_n=-\frac{2q_n(-1)^n\sinh(\kappa L)}{L(\kappa^2+q_n^2)}.
$$
For example, the required <antiderivatives> are $e^{\kappa x}(\kappa\cos q_nx+q_n\sin q_nx)/(\kappa^2+q_n^2)$ and $e^{\kappa x}(\kappa\sin q_nx-q_n\cos q_nx)/(\kappa^2+q_n^2)$. The <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> gives the exponential value away from the jumps and their midpoint $\cosh(\kappa L)$ at odd multiples of $L$. For $\kappa=0$, the <function> is identically one, with $a_0=2$ and all other <Fourier coefficients> zero.

= Fourier series of a half-wave rectified sinusoid
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}

For $V(t)=V_0\max(\sin\omega t,0)$, with period $2\pi/\omega$, <half-wave rectification> gives
$$
V(t)=\frac{V_0}{\pi}+\frac{V_0}{2}\sin\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{\pi}\sum_{m=1}^\infty\frac{\cos(2m\omega t)}{4m^2-1}.
$$
The <Fourier coefficients> are $a_0=2V_0/\pi$, $b_1=V_0/2$, $a_{2m}=-2V_0/[\pi(4m^2-1)]$, with all remaining coefficients zero. To derive the cosine coefficients, integrate $\sin s\cos ns$ over $0<s<\pi$ and use the product-to-sum identity. The sine coefficients follow from <orthogonality>. A half-period delay reverses only the fundamental sine term and leaves the constant and even-frequency cosine terms unchanged, by <translation of a Fourier series>.

= Translation of a Fourier series
{parent=Fourier series}

For a period-$L$ <Fourier series> written as $a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}(a_n\cos k_nx+b_n\sin k_nx)$, where $k_n=2\pi n/L$, translating the <function> by $\ell$ to $g(x)=f(x-\ell)$ rotates each pair of <Fourier coefficients>:
$$
a_n^{(g)}=a_n\cos(k_n\ell)-b_n\sin(k_n\ell),\qquad
b_n^{(g)}=a_n\sin(k_n\ell)+b_n\cos(k_n\ell).
$$
The constant coefficient is unchanged. These formulas follow by the sine and cosine subtraction identities. In the <complex Fourier series>, the same statement is $\widehat g_n=e^{-ik_n\ell}\widehat f_n$. An integer-period translation changes no <Fourier coefficient>.

= Fourier series of periodic hyperbolic sine and cosine
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}

For $\beta>0$, periodically extend $\cosh(\beta x)$ and $\sinh(\beta x)$ from $-1<x\leq1$ with period $2$. Their <Fourier series> are
$$
\cosh(\beta x)=\frac{\sinh\beta}{\beta}+2\beta\sinh\beta\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n\cos(n\pi x)}{\beta^2+n^2\pi^2},
$$
and, away from odd integers,
$$
\sinh(\beta x)=2\pi\sinh\beta\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n\sin(n\pi x)}{\beta^2+n^2\pi^2}.
$$
The first identity follows by <integration> of $\cosh(\beta x)\cos(n\pi x)$ on $[-1,1]$, using the <antiderivative>
$$
\frac{\beta\sinh(\beta x)\cos(n\pi x)+n\pi\cosh(\beta x)\sin(n\pi x)}{\beta^2+n^2\pi^2}.
$$
For the second identity, <integration by parts> and $d\cosh(\beta x)/dx=\beta\sinh(\beta x)$ show that its sine coefficient is $-n\pi/\beta$ times the first series' cosine coefficient. This avoids assuming that a <Fourier series> can always be differentiated term by term. The periodic <hyperbolic cosine> is continuous, so its series converges to it everywhere. The periodic <hyperbolic sine> has a <jump discontinuity> at each odd integer: the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> gives the average of its two limiting values, namely zero, there. Both identities hold in the sense of <mean-square convergence>, which suffices for <Parseval's identity>.

= Square wave
{parent=Fourier series}
{wiki}
{title2=$\operatorname{sgn}(\sin\theta)=\frac4\pi\sum_{n\geq1,\ n\text{ odd}}\frac{\sin n\theta}{n}$}

A <square wave> alternates between two constant levels for equal halves of a <period>. Choose a $2\pi$-periodic <function> equal to $1$ on $(0,\pi)$ and $-1$ on $(-\pi,0)$, with value zero at the jumps. Its <odd function> symmetry makes the mean and every cosine <Fourier coefficient> vanish. Directly integrating the two constant pieces gives
$$
a_0=a_n=0,\qquad b_n=\frac{2[1-(-1)^n]}{\pi n},\qquad
f(x)\sim\frac4\pi\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{\sin((2k+1)x)}{2k+1}.
$$
Thus only <odd> sine <Fourier harmonics> occur, with amplitudes decreasing like the reciprocal of their frequency multiplier. Every finite <Fourier partial sum> is smooth, yet their <pointwise limit> has jumps. At those jumps the <Fourier series> converges to zero, the midpoint of the two levels, regardless of which value we assign to the original <function> there.

This example explains how smooth components can encode sharp transitions. Increasing the number of <Fourier harmonics> narrows the transition region, but also produces the persistent overshoot described by the <Gibbs phenomenon>. The illustrated square wave and coefficient calculation are mathematical constructions, not measurements of a physical device.

= Gibbs phenomenon
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}
{wiki}

The <Gibbs phenomenon> is the persistent overshoot near a <jump discontinuity> in ordinary truncated <Fourier series>. For this <square wave>, retain $M$ nonzero odd modes:
$$
Q_M(x)=\frac4\pi\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\frac{\sin((2k+1)x)}{2k+1}.
$$
As $M$ grows, the first peak approaches the jump while its height tends to approximately $1.17897974$. This is an excess of about $0.17898$ above the upper level $1$, or $8.949\%$ of the jump size $2$. The narrower region does not imply a vanishing maximum error. At the jump itself every $Q_M(0)=0$. These observations are compatible with <pointwise convergence> and $L^2$ convergence but exclude <uniform convergence> to the discontinuous <square wave>. https://dlmf.nist.gov/6.16[NIST DLMF, section 6.16(i): square-wave overshoot and its limiting height]{ref}.

\Image[square-wave-fourier-ringing.png]
{title=More Fourier harmonics narrow a transition while leaving a persistent overshoot}
{description=Left: the constructed <square wave> and sums of $3$, $10$ and $50$ nonzero odd <Fourier harmonics>. Right: the same sums near the jump at zero, with each first positive peak marked. The highest frequency retained is $2M-1$, so a count of $M$ nonzero terms differs from a cutoff at frequency $M$. The limiting peak is approximately $1.17898$, while the level above the jump is $1$.}

For this particular sum, its <derivative> is $Q_M'(x)=(2/\pi)\sin(2Mx)/\sin x$ away from multiples of $\pi$, so the first positive peak is exactly at $x=\pi/(2M)$. There,
$$
Q_M\!\left(\frac\pi{2M}\right)
=\frac2M\sum_{k=0}^{M-1}\frac{\sin(\pi u_k)}{\pi u_k},\qquad u_k=\frac{k+1/2}{M}.
$$
This is a midpoint <Riemann sum> tending to $2\operatorname{Si}(\pi)/\pi$, where $\operatorname{Si}$ is the <sine integral>. It gives a short explanation of the nonzero limiting excess.

The phenomenon matters whenever a sharp boundary is represented by a limited set of <Fourier harmonics>: a visible ripple can be a property of the approximation rather than an oscillation in the original profile. <Josiah Willard Gibbs> reported the phenomenon in his 1899 letter “Fourier's Series” in Nature, volume 59, page 606. This is a documented historical report, without a claim of first discovery. https://www.nature.com/articles/059606a0[Gibbs, Fourier's Series, 1899]{ref}.

= Fourier series of a noninteger-frequency sine
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$\sin(\lambda x)\sim\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}n\sin(\pi\lambda)}{\pi(n^2-\lambda^2)}\sin(nx)$}

For real noninteger $\lambda$, the periodic extension from $[-\pi,\pi]$ has the displayed <Fourier series>, obtained by the product-to-sum identity in the sine coefficient integral. It equals the original function in the open interval and zero at the periodic endpoint jumps. At $\lambda=1/2$, <Parseval's identity> gives $\sum_{n\geq1}n^2/(4n^2-1)^2=\pi^2/64$. Integer frequencies instead already belong to the ordinary sine basis and should not be substituted into the removable coefficient singularities without taking limits.

= Fourier orthogonality
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}e^{i(n-m)t}\,dt=\delta_{nm}$}

The <complex exponential functions> $e^{int}$, for <integers> $n$, are <orthogonal> under the normalized <integral> <inner product> over a complete <period>. If $n=m$, the integrand $e^{i(n-m)t}$ is one; otherwise its <antiderivative> has equal endpoint values, giving zero. For the real modes, $\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\cos^2(nt)\,dt=\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\sin^2(nt)\,dt=\pi$ for $n\geq1$, while every mixed sine-cosine integral and every unequal-frequency pairing vanishes.

This makes the coefficient formulas useful: multiply a <trigonometric polynomial> by one mode and integrate, and all other modes cancel. For $f(x)=1/2+\cos x+\tfrac12\sin(2x)$, integration against $\cos x$ gives $\pi$, and against $\sin(2x)$ gives $\pi/2$, recovering $a_1=1$ and $b_2=1/2$. Division by the squared <norm> of the mode supplies the normalization. A full <period> matters; truncating the integration interval generally destroys these cancellations.

= Uniqueness of a trigonometric series outside a finite set
{parent=Fourier series}

If the symmetric partial sums of $\sum_{r\in\mathbb Z}a_re^{irt}$ tend to zero at every point outside a finite set, then all coefficients vanish. Almost-everywhere convergence first gives $a_{\pm n}\to0$. The twice-integrated series $F(t)=a_0t^2/2-\sum_{r\ne0}a_re^{irt}/r^2$ is continuous. <Riemann summation of a convergent trigonometric series> makes its symmetric second derivative zero away from the exceptional points, so it is affine on each intervening interval. <Sinc-squared summation of a sequence tending to zero> makes its symmetric first-order quotient zero everywhere, removing all possible affine corners. The globally affine function differs from a periodic function by $a_0t^2/2$, forcing $a_0=0$; periodicity then makes it constant, and integration against each nonzero frequency gives $a_r=0$.

= Riemann summation of a convergent trigonometric series
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$\sum_r a_re^{irt}\operatorname{sinc}^2(rh/2)\to\sum_r a_re^{irt}$}

Suppose the symmetric partial sums converge at a fixed point. Group positive and negative frequencies together, let $S_n$ be the resulting partial sums and set $w(x)=(\sin x/x)^2$, with $w(0)=1$. <Summation by parts> expresses the weighted sum as $\sum_{n\ge0}S_n[w(nk)-w((n+1)k)]$, where $k=|h|/2$. The total variation of these weights is at most $\int_0^\infty|w'(x)|dx<\infty$, independently of $k$, and each fixed difference tends to zero. Subtract the limiting value of $S_n$ and split into a finite prefix and a uniformly small tail to prove convergence to that value. This is a regular summation method even though the sinc-squared weights need not decrease monotonically.

= Decay of coefficients of an almost-everywhere convergent trigonometric series
{parent=Fourier series}

If symmetric partial sums $\sum_{r=-n}^n a_re^{irt}$ converge almost everywhere on the circle, then $a_n,a_{-n}\to0$. Their successive differences $T_n=a_ne^{int}+a_{-n}e^{-int}$ tend to zero almost everywhere. If $M_n=|a_n|+|a_{-n}|$ failed to tend to zero, choose a subsequence with $M_n\ge\varepsilon$. The normalized $T_n/M_n$ are bounded by one and tend to zero almost everywhere, so <dominated convergence> makes their squared integrals tend to zero. <Fourier orthogonality> instead makes those integrals $(|a_n|^2+|a_{-n}|^2)/M_n^2\ge1/2$, a contradiction.

= Periodic triangular bump
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$b_\varepsilon(t)=(1-\|t\|/\varepsilon)_+$}

For $0<\varepsilon\le1/2$, this continuous function on $\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$ has nonnegative <Fourier coefficients>
$$
\widehat b_\varepsilon(r)=\varepsilon\left(\frac{\sin(\pi r\varepsilon)}{\pi r\varepsilon}\right)^2,
\qquad \widehat b_\varepsilon(0)=\varepsilon.
$$
It is $\varepsilon^{-1}$ times the periodic <convolution> of two indicators of $[-\varepsilon/2,\varepsilon/2]$. Its <Fourier series> is absolutely convergent, $\sum_r\widehat b_\varepsilon(r)=b_\varepsilon(0)=1$, and $\sum_{|r|>R}\widehat b_\varepsilon(r)\ll1/(\varepsilon R)$. Consequently, if all $N$ points $t_n$ avoid the interval $\|t\|<\varepsilon$, some frequency $1\le r\ll\varepsilon^{-2}$ satisfies $|\sum_ne(rt_n)|\gg N\varepsilon$. This detects missing small neighborhoods by a large <exponential sum>.

= Du Bois-Reymond theorem
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}

There are <continuous functions> whose <Fourier series> diverges at a prescribed point. In contrast, the <Fejér kernel> is nonnegative and has integral one, so <Fejér summation is a uniform-norm contraction>. The resulting <Fejér sums> need not be positive: for the <constant function> $f=-1$, every sum is $-1$.

= Smooth-window Fourier coefficients of a periodic distribution
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$c_m=\widehat{\chi f}(2\pi m)$}

A smooth compactly supported window with $\sum_{j\in\mathbb Z}\chi(x+j)=1$ permits multiplying every periodic <distribution> unambiguously. Its <Fourier transform> sampled at integer harmonics gives the periodic coefficients. The sharp window $H(x)-H(x-1)$ works for locally integrable functions but can be undefined at singular period endpoints, such as delta derivatives.

= Fourier coefficients of a periodic logarithmic singularity
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$c_m\sim-(-1)^m/(2m)$}

For the period-one continuation of $\log|x-1/2|$, symmetry and <integration by parts> give $c_m=-(-1)^m\operatorname{Si}(\pi m)/(\pi m)$ for $m\ne0$. Thus the <sine integral> asymptotic gives $c_m=-(-1)^m/(2m)+1/(\pi^2m^2)+O(m^{-4})$ as $m\to+\infty$. Translation of the logarithmic singularity causes the alternating phase; the endpoint derivative mismatch causes the next term.

= Cohomological equation on a Diophantine torus
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$\mathcal D_\omega f=g$}

= Cohomological equations on a Diophantine torus
{synonym}

For a <Diophantine frequency vector>, the equation $\omega\cdot\partial_x f=g$ on a periodic <torus> has an analytic solution on each smaller complex strip exactly when $g$ has zero average. Its nonzero <Fourier coefficients> are $f_k=g_k/(i k\cdot\omega)$, and its zero coefficient is arbitrary. Thus the normalized zero-mean solution is unique.

= Translated conjugacy of a Diophantine vector field
{parent=Cohomological equation on a Diophantine torus}
{title2=$D\chi\,\omega=\omega+w\circ\chi+a$}

For a sufficiently small analytic periodic <vector field> $w$, a near-identity angular <diffeomorphism> $\chi$ and a constant vector $a$ solve $D\chi\,\omega=\omega+w\circ\chi+a$. They satisfy $\chi-\mathrm{id}=O(w)$, $a=O(w)$; if $\langle w\rangle=0$, then $a=O(w^2)$. For the defect $e=\mathcal D_\omega\chi-\omega-w\circ\chi-a$, set $A=D\chi$, choose $\Delta a=\langle A^{-1}\rangle^{-1}\langle A^{-1}e\rangle$, and solve $\mathcal D_\omega v=A^{-1}(\Delta a-e)$ with zero mean. Updating $\chi$ by $Av$ leaves a quadratic defect $(De)v-[w(\chi+Av)-w(\chi)-Dw(\chi)Av]$. The <torus small-divisor estimate> and geometrically decreasing strip losses yield a convergent analytic iteration.

= Analytic estimate for the torus cohomological equation
{parent=Cohomological equation on a Diophantine torus}
{title2=$\|f\|_{\sigma-\delta}\le C\gamma^{-1}\delta^{-(n+\tau)}\|g\|_\sigma$}

= Torus small-divisor estimate
{synonym}

If $g$ is analytic on a complex strip of width $\sigma$ and has zero mean, the normalized solution satisfies $\|f\|_{\sigma-\delta}\le C_{n,\tau}\gamma^{-1}\delta^{-(n+\tau)}\|g\|_\sigma$. Fourier decay supplies $e^{-\delta|k|_1}$ after the strip loss, and lattice-shell counting bounds the remaining sum $\sum_{k\ne0}|k|_1^\tau e^{-\delta|k|_1}$. This simple bound is sufficient for analytic conjugacy iterations even when sharper estimates are available.

= Kahane-Katznelson divergence theorem
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}

= Kahane and Katznelson theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Every null subset of the circle lies in the divergence set of the <Fourier series> of some continuous complex-valued <function>. One can arrange unbounded <Fourier partial sums> there. The <compact-set Fourier amplification lemma>, <compact batching of a small open set> and <frequency-separated Fourier block series> construct the function by a summable series of small blocks. The result covers nonclosed and dense null sets; it does not require the divergence set to equal the originally specified set.

= Orthogonality of integer Fourier modes
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$\int_0^1e^{2\pi imx}\,dx=1_{m=0}$}

For an integer $m$, direct integration gives zero unless $m=0$, in which case it gives one. Products of this identity on the unit cube turn <integrals> of finite <exponential sums> into counts of integer solutions. This is the elementary orthogonality used in the <Vinogradov mean value>.

= Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}

The <Fourier series> of a periodic function of <bounded variation> converges at each point to the mean of its two one-sided limits. In particular, it converges to the function at continuity points. Periodizing a compact interval introduces jumps at its integer endpoints; their half-values explain the half-weight convention in the <Van der Corput sum-integral lemma>. The conclusion is pointwise convergence, not a claim of <absolute convergence> of every such Fourier series.

= Fourier series of a periodically extended quadratic arch
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{title2=$f_{\rm per}(x)=\frac16-\frac1{\pi^2}\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{\cos(2\pi nx)}{n^2}$}

The period-one extension of $x(1-x)$ on $[0,1]$ has <Fourier series> $1/6-\pi^{-2}\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-2}\cos(2\pi nx)$. Endpoint agreement makes the periodic function continuous. Its derivative series is $(2/\pi)\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-1}\sin(2\pi nx)$, which equals $1-2x$ in the open unit interval and converges to zero at integers, the midpoint of the derivative's jump. <Termwise differentiation of a Fourier series> applies locally away from the corners.

= Pointwise convergence of a piecewise smooth Fourier series
{parent=Fourier series}

For a periodic <function> that is piecewise continuously differentiable with finitely many pieces, its <Fourier series> converges at each point to the average of the two one-sided limits. At a point of continuity this is the function value. This includes the identified interval endpoints, whose two limits belong to the periodic extension. Absolute summability of the <Fourier coefficients> additionally gives uniform convergence.

= Complex Fourier series
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$f\sim\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}c_ne^{in\pi x/L}$}

The complex form of a <Fourier series> for period $2L$ uses coefficients $c_n=(2L)^{-1}\int_{-L}^Lf(x)e^{-in\pi x/L}dx$ and complex exponentials $e^{in\pi x/L}$. For <square-integrable functions> it converges in the $L^2$ norm, and the <Parseval identity> is $(2L)^{-1}\int|f|^2=\sum_n|c_n|^2$. Real functions satisfy $c_{-n}=\overline{c_n}$.

= Reciprocal-square sine series positivity
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$S(\xi)=\sum_{l\ge1}\sin(l\xi)/l^2$}

The absolutely convergent sine series $S(\xi)=\sum_{l\ge1}\sin(l\xi)/l^2$ has the integral representation $S(\xi)=\sin\xi\int_0^\infty t e^{-t}/(1-2e^{-t}\cos\xi+e^{-2t})\,dt$. The integrand is positive for $0<\xi<\pi$, proving $S>0$ there without incorrectly maximizing individual Fourier terms.

= Wiener algebra
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$A(\mathbb T)$}

The Wiener algebra consists of periodic <functions> with absolutely summable <Fourier coefficients>, with <norm> $\sum_k|a_k|$. Multiplication corresponds to <discrete convolution> of coefficients, so the <norm> is submultiplicative. Consequently $e^f=\sum_{n\ge0}f^n/n!$ also belongs to the algebra when $f$ does. Every such <Fourier series> is uniformly convergent and defines a <continuous function>.

= Character space of the Wiener algebra
{parent=Wiener algebra}
{title2=$\Phi_{A(\mathbb T)}\cong\mathbb T$}

Every <algebra character> of the <Wiener algebra> is evaluation at a unique point of the <unit circle>. Writing $u(w)=w$, both $u$ and $u^{-1}$ have <norm> one. Thus $z=\phi(u)$ satisfies $|z|\leq1$ and $|z^{-1}|\leq1$, hence $|z|=1$. Continuity then gives $\phi(\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}a_nu^n)=\sum a_nz^n$. Conversely, evaluation defines a continuous <algebra character>. The resulting bijection is a <homeomorphism> for the <Gelfand topology>.

= Analytic Wiener algebra
{parent=Wiener algebra}
{title2=$A^+(\mathbb T)$}

The analytic Wiener algebra consists of the <power series> $f(z)=\sum_{n\geq0}a_nz^n$ with $\sum_{n\geq0}|a_n|<\infty$, identified with their values on the <unit circle>. The <norm> is $\|f\|=\sum|a_n|$. <Discrete convolution> gives multiplication and a submultiplicative <norm>; completeness follows from the <absolutely summable sequence space>. Each series converges uniformly on the closed <unit disk> and is <holomorphic> in its interior.

= Character space of the analytic Wiener algebra
{parent=Analytic Wiener algebra}
{title2=$\Phi_{A^+(\mathbb T)}\cong\overline{\mathbb D}$}

Every <algebra character> of the <analytic Wiener algebra> is evaluation at a unique point $z$ of the closed <unit disk>. Indeed, for $u(w)=w$, continuity and multiplicativity give $z=\phi(u)$, $|z|\leq\|u\|=1$ and $\phi(f)=\sum_{n\geq0}a_nz^n$. Conversely, this formula defines a continuous <algebra character> for every such $z$. The evaluation correspondence is a <homeomorphism> for the <Gelfand topology>, since the <power series> are uniformly convergent on the closed <unit disk>.

= Fourier harmonic
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}

A sinusoidal component at an integer multiple of a fundamental angular frequency. For a $2\pi$-periodic angular coordinate $\phi$, real <Fourier series> use $\cos(k\phi)$ and $\sin(k\phi)$. Summing over $q$ equally spaced phases removes every component whose integer $k$ is not a multiple of $q$, since $\sum_{j=0}^{q-1}e^{2\pi i k j/q}=0$ in that case. Indeed, the <geometric series> has ratio $w=e^{2\pi i k/q}\ne1$ and sum $(1-w^q)/(1-w)=0$. When $q$ divides $k$, all $q$ terms are one. This cancellation isolates higher-order feedback in <resonant conjunction geometry>.

= Cotangent partial-fraction Fourier kernel
{parent=Fourier series}
{title2=$\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}(z+n)^{-k}$}

For an integer $k\geq2$ and $\operatorname{Im}z>0$, differentiating the partial-fraction expansion of $\pi\cot\pi z$ gives
$$
\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}(z+n)^{-k}=\frac{(-2\pi i)^k}{(k-1)!}\sum_{r\geq1}r^{k-1}e^{2\pi irz}.
$$
Both differentiated series converge locally uniformly. This kernel computes the <Fourier expansion of a modular form> for holomorphic <Eisenstein series>.

= Cosecant partial-fraction identity
{parent=Cotangent partial-fraction Fourier kernel}
{title2=$\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}(w+n)^{-2}=\pi^2\csc^2(\pi w)$}

For $w\notin\mathbb Z$, integrate $\pi\cot(\pi\zeta)/(\zeta-w)^2$ around large squares whose sides have half-integer coordinates. The <cotangent> is uniformly bounded on these sides and the integral tends to zero. The <residue theorem> gives residues $(n-w)^{-2}$ at the integers and $-\pi^2\csc^2(\pi w)$ at $w$, proving the identity. For $\operatorname{Im}w>0$, the geometric-series formula for the <cotangent> also gives $\pi^2\csc^2(\pi w)=-4\pi^2\sum_{r\geq1}r e^{2\pi irw}$.

= Fourier basis
{parent=Fourier series}

On the circle of circumference $2\pi$, the functions $e_k(x)=e^{ikx}$ for $k\in\mathbb Z$ form an orthogonal basis of $L^2$.

= Fourier sine series
{parent=Fourier series}

= Sine series
{synonym}

A Fourier sine series expands a function on an interval in the eigenfunctions $\sin(n\pi x/L)$ of the Dirichlet Laplacian. It is the odd Fourier-series extension across the interval endpoints.

= Double Fourier sine series
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{title2=$f(x,y)=\sum_{m,n\geq1}c_{mn}\sin(m\pi x/L_x)\sin(n\pi y/L_y)$}

On a rectangle $(0,L_x)\times(0,L_y)$, products of the one-dimensional <Fourier sine basis> give an orthogonal basis of <L2 space>. <Fourier orthogonality> in each variable gives
$$
c_{mn}=\frac4{L_xL_y}\int_0^{L_x}\int_0^{L_y}f(x,y)\sin\frac{m\pi x}{L_x}\sin\frac{n\pi y}{L_y}\,dy\,dx.
$$
For a constant function one, these coefficients are $4[1-(-1)^m][1-(-1)^n]/(\pi^2mn)$: only odd indices occur. The product modes vanish on the rectangle's boundary, making them natural <Dirichlet Laplacian eigenfunctions>. In the <heat equation>, their amplitudes decay as $e^{-\kappa\pi^2(m^2/L_x^2+n^2/L_y^2)t}$. Initial data not vanishing at the boundary are still represented in the interior and in the square-integrable sense; the positive-time boundary values need not agree continuously with the initially assigned boundary values.

= Fourier sine series of a symmetric tent
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{c}
{title2=$\min(x,L-x)=\frac{4L}{\pi^2}\sum_{n\geq1}\frac{\sin(n\pi/2)}{n^2}\sin(n\pi x/L)$}

On $0\leq x\leq L$, with $L>0$, the tent $f(x)=\min(x,L-x)$ has <Fourier sine series> coefficients $b_n=4L\sin(n\pi/2)/(\pi^2n^2)$. Split the coefficient integral at $L/2$ and integrate its two linear pieces by parts; their cosine endpoint terms cancel and their sine terms add. Reflection about the midpoint makes every even-index coefficient zero, and the remaining odd-index coefficients alternate in sign. The $n^{-2}$ bound gives <absolute convergence> and <uniform convergence>; the series equals the continuous tent, including its zero endpoints.

= Sine expansion and Rayleigh quotient of a parabolic Dirichlet trial
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{title2=$x(1-x)=\frac8{\pi^3}\sum_{n\geq1,\ n\text{ odd}}\frac{\sin(n\pi x)}{n^3}$}

On $(0,1)$, integration by parts gives the displayed <Fourier sine series>. For normalized <Dirichlet eigenfunctions> $y_i=\sqrt2\sin[(i+1)\pi x]$, indexed from $i=0$, it has the form $A[y_0+\sum_{i\geq1}c_i y_i]$, with $A=4\sqrt2/\pi^3$ and $c_i=[1-(-1)^{i+1}]/[2(i+1)^3]$. Its <Rayleigh quotient> for $-d^2/dx^2$ is $10$, since $\int_0^1(u')^2dx=1/3$ and $\int_0^1u^2dx=1/30$. Reflection symmetry forces $c_1=0$, because that first excited eigenfunction is antisymmetric about $1/2$. Thus the first excited state's contribution to the error is exactly zero; the first nonzero correction is $c_2=1/27$.

= Fourier series of a periodic sawtooth
{c}
{parent=Fourier sine series}

The period-$L$ extension of $f(x)=Kx/L$ on $-L/2<x<L/2$ has <Fourier coefficients> $a_0=a_n=0$ and $b_n=K(-1)^{n+1}/(\pi n)$. Consequently
$$
f(x)\sim\frac K\pi\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n\sin\frac{2\pi nx}{L}.
$$
The <Fourier series> converges to the ramp away from its jumps and to zero at every jump, the average of the one-sided limits. For $K\ne0$, <termwise differentiation of a Fourier series> fails here as an ordinary convergent series: the differentiated summands have nondecaying amplitude $2K/L$. The classical derivative equals $K/L$ between jumps, whereas the periodic <distributional derivative> includes negative point masses of weight $K$ at the jumps.

= Fourier sine series of a squared sine
{c}
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{title2=$\sin^2(\pi x/L)=\frac8\pi\sum_{m\geq0}\frac{\sin((2m+1)\pi x/L)}{(2m+1)[4-(2m+1)^2]}$}

On $0\leq x\leq L$, the <Fourier sine series> of $\sin^2(\pi x/L)$ has zero even-index coefficients and odd-index coefficients $b_n=8/[\pi n(4-n^2)]$. Substitute $u=\pi x/L$ in the coefficient integral and use $\sin^2u\sin(nu)=\tfrac12\sin(nu)-\tfrac14\sin((n+2)u)-\tfrac14\sin((n-2)u)$. For odd $n$, integrating gives $b_n=(2/\pi)[1/n-\tfrac12(1/(n+2)+1/(n-2))]$; reflection about the midpoint makes the even coefficients zero. The $O(n^{-3})$ decay gives absolute and uniform convergence, and <Fourier orthogonality> identifies the sum. In the <heat equation> with zero endpoint temperatures, multiply each coefficient by $\exp[-\kappa(n\pi/L)^2t]$. With insulated endpoints, the same initial profile is instead just the constant and second cosine modes of a <Fourier cosine series>.

= Fourier sine series of an odd quadratic arch
{c}
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{title2=$x(L-|x|)=\frac{8L^2}{\pi^3}\sum_{m\geq0}\frac{\sin((2m+1)\pi x/L)}{(2m+1)^3}$}

Extend $F_L(x)=x(L-|x|)$ from $[-L,L]$ to a <periodic function> of period $2L$, with $L>0$. It is odd and continuous, so its <Fourier series> contains only sine terms. Its <Fourier coefficients> are
$$
b_n=\frac2L\int_0^Lx(L-x)\sin(n\pi x/L)\,dx
=\frac{4L^2(1-(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3}.
$$
Two applications of <integration by parts> prove the coefficient formula. The first has no endpoint contribution because $x(L-x)$ vanishes at both endpoints; the second leaves a multiple of $1-\cos(n\pi)$. Thus only odd frequencies survive, giving the displayed <Fourier sine series>. Its absolutely summable coefficients give uniform convergence, and <Fourier orthogonality> identifies its sum with $F_L$.

Taking $L=1$, $\int_{-1}^1F_1(x)^2dx=2\int_0^1x^2(1-x)^2dx=1/15$. <Parseval's identity> therefore gives
$$
\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac1{(2m+1)^6}=\frac{\pi^6}{960}.
$$

= Uniform bound for harmonic sine polynomials
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{title2=$\sup_m\left\|\sum_{k=1}^m\frac{\sin kt}{k}\right\|_\infty\leq1+\pi$}

For $0<t\leq\pi$, split the sum at $\lfloor1/t\rfloor$. The first part is bounded using $|\sin kt|\leq kt$; <summation by parts> and the geometric bound on interval sine sums control the rest. The harmonic coefficient sum grows like $\log m$, but cancellations keep the whole <trigonometric polynomial> uniformly bounded. Modulating this polynomial lets a <Fourier partial sum> expose the large uncancelled half.

= Fourier sine basis
{parent=Fourier sine series}
{c}
{title2=$e_n(x)=\sqrt{2/\pi}\sin(nx)$}

The functions $\sqrt{2/\pi}\sin(nx)$, $n\geq1$, form a complete <orthonormal basis> of $L^2(0,\pi)$. An odd extension to $(-\pi,\pi)$ reduces completeness to that of the <Fourier series>. Equality of all sine coefficients therefore implies equality almost everywhere; if both functions are continuous, it implies equality everywhere.

= Leibniz formula for π
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}
{wiki}

The Leibniz formula is the conditionally convergent alternating series
$$
\frac\pi4=\sum_{r=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^r}{2r+1}.
$$

= Trigonometric polynomial
{parent=Fourier series}
{wiki}

A trigonometric polynomial is a finite linear combination of complex exponentials $e^{inx}$, or equivalently of sines and cosines. If it vanishes on an interval, all its coefficients vanish.

= Bernstein inequality for trigonometric polynomials
{c}
{parent=Trigonometric polynomial}
{title2=$\|T_n'\|_\infty\le n\|T_n\|_\infty$}

A <trigonometric polynomial> of degree at most $n$ obeys the displayed derivative bound. Iteration gives $\|T_n^{(r)}\|_\infty\le n^r\|T_n\|_\infty$. Applied to differences of approximants at dyadic degrees, this inequality underlies the <inverse theorem for trigonometric approximation>. It differs from the endpoint-weighted <Bernstein inequality for algebraic polynomials>.

= Gram matrix representation of a trigonometric polynomial
{parent=Trigonometric polynomial}
{title2=$p_k=\sum_{i-j=k}M_{ij}$}

Under the displayed diagonal-sum convention, set $w(z)=(1,z^{-1},\ldots,z^{-d})^T$ on the <unit circle>. A <Hermitian matrix> $M$ then gives
$$
p(z)=w(z)^*Mw(z).
$$
Consequently $M\succeq0$ proves nonnegativity. Conversely the <Fejér–Riesz theorem> produces the <rank-one spectral-factor Gram matrix>. This gives a <semidefinite programming> representation of nonnegative trigonometric <polynomials>. The reversed convention $j-i=k$ instead uses the unconjugated monomial <vector>.

= Rank-one spectral-factor Gram matrix
{parent=Gram matrix representation of a trigonometric polynomial}
{title2=$M=qq^*$}

For <coefficient> column $q=(q_0,\ldots,q_d)^T$, $M_{ij}=q_i\overline{q_j}$ is a <Hermitian matrix> and a <positive semidefinite matrix> because $x^*Mx=|q^*x|^2$. Expanding $|q(z)|^2$ on the <unit circle> gives <coefficient> $p_k=\sum_{i-j=k}M_{ij}$. Thus $M$ has <matrix rank> one when $q\ne0$ and zero otherwise. General feasible <Gram matrices> need not have <matrix rank> one.

= Fejér–Riesz theorem
{c}
{parent=Trigonometric polynomial}
{title2=$p(z)=|q(z)|^2\quad(|z|=1)$}

= Nonnegative trigonometric polynomial factorization
{synonym}

A <trigonometric polynomial> nonnegative on the <unit circle> has a <polynomial> modulus-square factor of <polynomial degree> at most its trigonometric order. For a nonzero <trigonometric polynomial> of actual order $d$, <conjugate symmetry of trigonometric polynomial coefficients> yields <reciprocal-conjugate root pairing> for $P=z^dp$. <Even multiplicity of unit-circle roots of a nonnegative trigonometric polynomial> permits pairing all $2d$ <roots of a polynomial>. Select representatives $\zeta_1,\ldots,\zeta_d$ and use
$$
z-\frac1{\overline{\zeta_i}}
=-\frac z{\overline{\zeta_i}}(\overline z-\overline{\zeta_i})
\quad(|z|=1).
$$
It follows that $p(z)=c\prod_i|z-\zeta_i|^2$ with $c=(-1)^dp_d/\prod_i\overline{\zeta_i}$. Evaluating away from the <roots of a polynomial> gives $c>0$. Thus $q(z)=\sqrt c\prod_i(z-\zeta_i)$ works. Constant and zero <trigonometric polynomials> have constant or zero factors. Different selections of <roots of a polynomial> and constant phases can give different factors.

= Even multiplicity of unit-circle roots of a nonnegative trigonometric polynomial
{parent=Trigonometric polynomial}

If $P=z^dp$ and $p$ is nonnegative on the <unit circle>, a <root of a polynomial> $\zeta=e^{i\theta_0}$ there has even <multiplicity of a root>. Indeed $p(e^{i\theta})$ is a <real analytic> nonnegative function, whose first nonzero term in its <Taylor series> at a zero must have even order. The map $\theta\mapsto e^{i\theta}-\zeta$ has a simple zero, and multiplication by $e^{-id\theta}$ is nonvanishing, so the order equals the <polynomial> multiplicity.

= Conjugate symmetry of trigonometric polynomial coefficients
{parent=Trigonometric polynomial}
{title2=$p_{-k}=\overline{p_k}$}

A <trigonometric polynomial> is real on the <unit circle> precisely when its finite <coefficients> have the displayed symmetry. To prove necessity, conjugate its values on the circle, use $\overline z=z^{-1}$, and compare <coefficients>. Multiplying a <coefficient> difference by $z^d$ gives an ordinary <polynomial> vanishing at infinitely many points, so it is zero. Symmetry also gives $p(z^{-1})=\overline{p(\overline z)}$ for every nonzero complex $z$.

= Reciprocal-conjugate root pairing
{parent=Conjugate symmetry of trigonometric polynomial coefficients}

For a nonzero real-on-the-circle <trigonometric polynomial> of actual order $d$, the <polynomial> $P(z)=z^dp(z)$ satisfies $P(z)=z^{2d}\overline{P(1/\overline z)}$. Its constant term is the nonzero conjugate of its leading <coefficient>. Hence its nonzero <roots of a polynomial> occur in pairs $\zeta,1/\overline\zeta$ with the same <multiplicity of a root>. The <coefficient> reversal and <complex conjugation> preserve the exponents of the paired factors. Fixed <roots of a polynomial> on the <unit circle> require nonnegativity, rather than this symmetry alone, to have even multiplicity.

= Fourier partial sum
{c}
{title2=$s_n(f)$}
{parent=Fourier series}

A <Fourier partial sum> retains the modes whose <integer> frequency multipliers satisfy $|k|\leq n$ in a <complex Fourier series>:
$$
S_n f(x)=\sum_{k=-n}^{n}\widehat f_k e^{ikx}.
$$
For a real <function> of period $2\pi$, this is equivalently $a_0/2+\sum_{k=1}^{n}[a_k\cos(kx)+b_k\sin(kx)]$. It is a <trigonometric polynomial>, even when the original <function> is discontinuous. For a <square wave>, retaining $M$ nonzero odd sine modes gives $S_{2M-1}f$; all even-frequency coefficients are zero. The <Gibbs phenomenon> illustrates why more retained modes need not reduce the maximum error.

= Generic unbounded Fourier sums at a fixed point
{parent=Fourier partial sum}

For each integer $m\geq1$, the set of continuous functions satisfying $|S_N(f,0)|\leq m$ for every $N$ is closed in the <uniform norm>. If it contained a ball of radius $r$, subtracting two members of that ball would bound every partial-sum functional norm by $4m/r$. The <Dirichlet kernel harmonic lower bound> excludes this, so these sets are <nowhere dense>. By the <Baire category theorem>, their complement is a dense $G_\delta$ set. Every function in that complement has unbounded Fourier partial sums at zero. Translation gives the same conclusion at any prescribed point. This is a direct <uniform boundedness principle> argument on the real <Banach space> of continuous functions on the circle.

= de la Vallée Poussin sum
{c}
{parent=Fourier partial sum}
{title2=$v_{n,m}f=\frac1m\sum_{j=n}^{n+m-1}s_jf$}

= de la Vallée Poussin mean
{c}
{synonym}

This average of <Fourier partial sums> reproduces every degree-at-most-$n$ <trigonometric polynomial>. With $\sigma_r=r^{-1}\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}s_j$,
$$
v_{n,m}=\frac{(n+m)\sigma_{n+m}-n\sigma_n}{m},\qquad
\|v_{n,m}\|_\infty\le1+\frac{2n}{m}.
$$
The bound follows because <Fejér summation is a uniform-norm contraction>. For $n=0$, the term involving $\sigma_0$ is omitted.

= Bounded nonconvergent Fourier partial sums
{parent=Fourier partial sum}

A continuous complex-valued <function> can have uniformly bounded <Fourier partial sums> which fail to converge at one point. Normalize harmonic sine polynomials by their harmonic coefficient sums, modulate them into disjoint high-frequency intervals and choose their degrees so that the complete norms are summable. At zero, complete blocks vanish while their midpoint prefixes have a common nonzero value. The <frequency-separated Fourier block series> gives a uniform bound on every partial sum as well as two distinct subsequential values.

= Frequency-separated Fourier block series
{parent=Fourier partial sum}
{title2=$f=\sum_j q_j,\qquad \sum_j\|q_j\|_\infty<\infty$}

If <trigonometric polynomials> have spectra in increasing disjoint positive-frequency intervals, the displayed summability gives a continuous uniform sum. At any cutoff its <Fourier partial sum> contains complete earlier blocks, at most one active prefix and no later blocks. Differences across an active prefix therefore isolate that block exactly. Fixed-size excursions occurring in arbitrarily late blocks contradict the Cauchy criterion, even when the complete blocks vanish at the point under consideration.

= Compact-set Fourier amplification lemma
{parent=Fourier partial sum}
{title2=$\|q\|_\infty\leq\varepsilon,\quad |S_Lq|>M\ {\rm on}\ K$}

For a compact circle set with normalized <Lebesgue measure> at most $\exp(-8\pi M/\varepsilon)$, where $M\geq1$ and $0<\varepsilon\leq1$, the displayed bounds can be realized by a <trigonometric polynomial> with spectrum in a positive-frequency interval arbitrarily far from zero. Take the logarithm of a positive-real-part <Schwarz integral on the unit disk> of a small-mean cutoff, truncate after radial dilation, and modulate its bounded imaginary part. A prefix isolates one analytic half and thus reveals the large real part. The separation between small function norm and large <Fourier partial sums> drives the <Kahane-Katznelson divergence theorem>.

= Positive-real logarithmic amplifier for short arcs
{parent=Compact-set Fourier amplification lemma}
{title2=$\Phi(z)=\sum_j\frac{\delta_j}{D}\frac{1+\delta_j}{1+\delta_j-ze^{-i\theta_j}}$}

Let finitely many arcs have centers $\theta_j$, half-lengths $0<\delta_j\leq1$ and $D=\sum_j\delta_j$. Each summand has positive real part on the closed disk, and $\Phi(0)=1$. On the $j$th arc its contribution has real part at least $1/(4D)$. Thus the analytic logarithm $A=\log\Phi$ obeys $A(0)=0$, $|\operatorname{Im}A|<\pi/2$, and $\operatorname{Re}A\geq\log(1/(4D))$ throughout the arc union. Approximate $A$ by an analytic polynomial and modulate its bounded imaginary part; a partial Fourier sum isolates one half of the polynomial, producing a large value from a bounded function.

= Dirichlet kernel
{title2=$D_n$}
{c}
{parent=Fourier partial sum}
{wiki}

= Dirichlet kernel harmonic lower bound
{parent=Dirichlet kernel}
{c}
{title2=$\Lambda_N\geq\frac4{\pi^2}\sum_{j=1}^N1/j$}

For $D_N(t)=\sin((N+1/2)t)/\sin(t/2)$, define $\Lambda_N=(2\pi)^{-1}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}|D_N|$. Evenness and $\sin(t/2)\leq t/2$ on $(0,\pi]$ give $\Lambda_N\geq(2/\pi)\int_0^{(N+1/2)\pi}|\sin v|/v\,dv$. The first $N$ complete sine periods of length $\pi$ have absolute sine integral two, and on the $j$th such interval $1/v\geq1/(j\pi)$. This proves the displayed unbounded lower bound. The real functional $f\mapsto S_N(f,0)$ on continuous functions has norm exactly $\Lambda_N$: the upper bound is its kernel integral, and the continuous functions $D_N/\sqrt{D_N^2+\delta^2}$ approach that bound as $\delta\downarrow0$ by <dominated convergence>.

= Fejér kernel
{title2=$F_n$}
{c}
{parent=Fourier series}
{wiki}

The Fejér kernel is the arithmetic mean of the Dirichlet kernels. It is nonnegative and has normalized integral one.

= Fejér sum
{title2=$\sigma_n(f)$}
{c}
{parent=Fejér kernel}

The $n$th Fejér sum is the arithmetic mean of the first $n$ <Fourier partial sums>. Equivalently, it is the convolution of the function with the <Fejér kernel>.

= Hölder error bound for Fejér summation
{c}
{parent=Fejér sum}
{title2=$\|\sigma_{n-1}f-f\|_\infty\le C_\alpha M n^{-\alpha}$}

For a periodic <Hölder continuous function> of <Hölder exponent> $0<\alpha<1$ and <Hölder seminorm> at most $M$, the degree-$n-1$ <Fejér sum> has the displayed <supremum norm> error bound. In the half-kernel normalization $F_{n-1}=(2n)^{-1}\sin^2(nt/2)/\sin^2(t/2)$, use $F_{n-1}(t)\le\min\{n/2,\pi^2/(2nt^2)\}$. Splitting the convolution error integral at $1/n$ gives the valid constant $C_\alpha=1/[\pi(\alpha+1)]+\pi/(1-\alpha)$. At the endpoint $\alpha=1$ this argument instead gives $O(M\log n/n)$.

= Fejér first-moment approximation bound
{c}
{parent=Fejér sum}
{title2=$\|\sigma_nf-f\|_\infty\le C\omega(f,\log n/n)$}

The normalized <Fejér kernel> satisfies $0\le F_n(t)\le\min\{n/(2\pi),\pi/(2nt^2)\}$ on $0<|t|\le\pi$. Splitting its first absolute moment at $1/n$ gives $\int|t|F_n(t)dt\le1/(2\pi n)+(\pi/n)\log(\pi n)$. The chaining inequality for the <modulus of continuity>, $\omega(f,t)\le(1+t/\delta)\omega(f,\delta)$, then bounds the <Fejér sum> error by $\omega(f,\delta)[1+\delta^{-1}\int|t|F_n]$. Taking $\delta=\log n/n$ proves the stated bound for $n\ge2$ and hence <uniform convergence> for continuous periodic functions. The logarithm vanishes at $n=1$, so the displayed estimate is not intended at that index.

= Fractional-scale Fejér approximation bound
{parent=Fejér first-moment approximation bound}
{title2=$\|\sigma_nf-f\|_\infty\leq C_\alpha\omega(f,n^{-\alpha}),\quad0<\alpha<1$}

The positive normalized <Fejér kernel> has a $1/(nt^2)$ tail. Split the error integral at $\delta=n^{-\alpha}$ and use the chaining inequality $\omega(f,t)\leq(1+t/\delta)\omega(f,\delta)$. Its tail contributes at most a constant times $n^{\alpha-1}(1+\alpha\log n)\omega(f,\delta)$. The factor is uniformly bounded for each fixed $\alpha<1$, proving the displayed estimate. Its constant need not remain bounded as $\alpha$ tends to one.

= Fejér second-modulus approximation bound
{c}
{parent=Fejér sum}
{title2=$\|\sigma_nf-f\|_\infty\le(1+\pi^2)\omega_2(f,n^{-1/2})$}

The even positive <Fejér kernel> of total mass one gives $\sigma_nf-f=\tfrac12\int F_n(t)[f(\cdot+t)-2f+f(\cdot-t)]dt$. The scaling property of the <second modulus of smoothness> bounds this by $\omega_2(f,\delta)(1+\delta^{-2}\int t^2F_n(t)dt)$. On $[-\pi,\pi]$, $\sin(|t|/2)\ge |t|/\pi$ gives $\int t^2F_n(t)dt\le\pi^2/n$. Choose $\delta=n^{-1/2}$. For twice continuously differentiable functions, the <second-difference integral formula> makes the error $O(n^{-1})$.

= Fejér saturation on a Fourier mode
{c}
{parent=Fejér second-modulus approximation bound}
{title2=$\|\sigma_n(\cos kx)-\cos kx\|_\infty=k/n$}

For fixed positive integer $k$ and $n>k$, the <Fourier coefficient> of the <Fejér kernel> at frequency $k$ multiplies the mode by $1-k/n$. The displayed error is exact. Consequently the order $n^{-1}$ cannot be replaced by $o(n^{-1})$ for all twice continuously differentiable periodic functions, even though each fixed Fourier mode is infinitely differentiable.

= Fejér summation is a uniform-norm contraction
{c}
{parent=Fejér sum}
{title2=$\|\sigma_n f\|_\infty\le\|f\|_\infty$}

= Uniform-norm contraction of Fejér summation
{synonym}

The <Fejér kernel> is nonnegative and has unit mass in its convolution normalization. The integral triangle inequality therefore bounds the <supremum norm> of a <Fejér sum> by that of the original function. In the half-kernel convention $F_n=(2n)^{-1}|\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}e^{ijt}|^2$, its integral is $\pi$ and the convolution prefactor is $1/\pi$.

= Fourier coefficient
{title2=$\widehat f_n$}
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}

For an integrable <function> of <period> $L>0$, its complex <Fourier coefficients> measure its <inner product> with each <complex exponential> mode:
$$
\widehat f_n=\frac1L\int_{x_0}^{x_0+L}f(x)e^{-2\pi inx/L}\,dx.
$$
For a real <function> of period $2\pi$, the real coefficient convention of <Fourier series> gives $\widehat f_0=a_0/2$ and $\widehat f_n=(a_n-ib_n)/2$ for $n\geq1$, with $\widehat f_{-n}=\overline{\widehat f_n}$. For $f(x)=1/2+\cos x+\tfrac12\sin(2x)$, the only nonzero complex <Fourier coefficients> are $\widehat f_0=1/2$, $\widehat f_{\pm1}=1/2$, $\widehat f_2=-i/4$, and $\widehat f_{-2}=i/4$. This explains why the real and complex lists look different while representing the same <function>.

= Fourier sine coefficient
{parent=Fourier coefficient}
{c}
{title2=$b_n=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lf(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)dx$}

For an integrable <function> on $[-L,L]$, with $L>0$ and <integer> $n\geq1$, the displayed <Fourier coefficient> multiplies the mode $\sin(n\pi x/L)$ in its real <Fourier series>. <Fourier orthogonality> fixes the normalization because the squared mode has <integral> $L$. If $f$ is <odd>, the product with the sine is <even>, so $b_n=(2/L)\int_0^Lf(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)dx$. These are also the coefficients of the <Fourier sine series> of the restriction to $(0,L)$.

= Periodic dilation annihilates low Fourier modes
{parent=Fourier coefficient}
{title2=$\int_{\mathbb T}f(nx)e^{imx}\,dx=0\quad(0<|m|<n)$}

If $f$ is integrable and $2\pi$-periodic and $n$ is a positive integer, then $\int_0^{2\pi}f(nx)e^{imx}\,dx=0$ for every integer $m$ with $0<|m|<n$. Split the integral into $n$ equal intervals and substitute $y=nx-2\pi j$ to obtain
$$
\frac1n\int_0^{2\pi}f(y)e^{imy/n}\,dy
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}e^{2\pi imj/n}=0.
$$
The <root of unity> sum vanishes by the finite <geometric series> formula. The zero mode is $\int f$, so a mean-zero dilated function is orthogonal to all <trigonometric polynomials> of degree at most $n-1$.

= Absolute Fourier convergence from a square-integrable derivative
{parent=Fourier coefficient}
{title2=$\sum_{n\ne0}|\widehat f(n)|\leq(\pi/\sqrt3)\|f'\|_2$}

For a periodic absolutely continuous <function> with derivative in $L^2$, <integration by parts> gives $\widehat{f'}(n)=in\widehat f(n)$. <Bessel's inequality> and the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> prove the displayed bound in the normalized circle norm. Adding the constant coefficient gives an absolutely and uniformly convergent <Fourier series>. Merely bounding individual coefficients by $O(1/|n|)$ would not establish absolute convergence.

= Uniqueness of Fourier coefficients in L1
{parent=Fourier coefficient}

If a periodic integrable function has all <Fourier coefficients> zero, it vanishes almost everywhere. Its <Fejér sums> vanish, whereas the <Fejér kernel> is an approximate identity and converges to that function in the integrable norm. Applied to a periodized squared <Fourier transform>, this detects orthogonality of integer translates.

= Fourier coefficient decay of an analytic periodic function
{c}
{parent=Fourier coefficient}

If a periodic function extends analytically and remains bounded in a nonzero complex strip around the real axis, contour shifting gives constants $C,c>0$ such that
$$
|\widehat f_n|\leq Ce^{-c|n|}.
$$
This exponential decay underlies spectral convergence of Fourier approximation and periodic trapezoidal quadrature.

= Termwise differentiation of a Fourier series
{c}
{parent=Fourier coefficient}

When regularity and convergence justify termwise differentiation, the derivative of a Fourier mode multiplies its coefficient by its frequency. For period $L$,
$$
\widehat{f'}_n=\frac{2\pi in}{L}\widehat f_n.
$$

= Fourier cosine series
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}

A Fourier cosine series has the form
$$
\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_n\cos(nx).
$$
It represents the <even function> obtained by reflecting its data across the origin.

= Half-range Fourier cosine series
{parent=Fourier cosine series}
{title2=$f(x)=a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}a_n\cos(n\pi x/L)$}

For a function prescribed on $0<x<L$, its half-range <Fourier cosine series> is the <Fourier series> of the even extension, made $2L$-periodic. Its coefficients are $a_n=(2/L)\int_0^L f(x)\cos(n\pi x/L)\,dx$, including $n=0$. At a jump the usual piecewise-smooth convergence theorem gives the average of the two one-sided limits of the periodic extension.

= Fourier cosine coefficients of a sine mode
{c}
{parent=Half-range Fourier cosine series}
{title2=$a_n=4m/[\pi(m^2-n^2)]\text{ when }m,n\text{ have opposite parity}$}

For a positive integer $m$, take $f(x)=\sin(mx)$ on $0<x<\pi$ and form its <half-range Fourier cosine series>. The product-to-sum identity gives
$$
a_n=\frac1\pi\left[\frac{1-(-1)^{m+n}}{m+n}+\frac{1-(-1)^{m-n}}{m-n}\right],\qquad n\geq0,\ n\ne m.
$$
Thus $a_n=4m/[\pi(m^2-n^2)]$ for opposite parity and zero for the same parity. The exceptional integral $a_m=(2/\pi)\int_0^\pi\sin(mx)\cos(mx)dx$ is zero. The constant term is $a_0/2$, not $a_0$. These coefficients represent the even periodic extension, not the original sine wave on negative arguments. Their quadratic decay gives <absolute convergence>.

= Fourier series of x cubed minus pi squared x
{parent=Fourier series}
{c}

On $(-\pi,\pi)$,
$$
x^3-\pi^2x
=12\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{n^3}\sin(nx).
$$
Parseval's identity and direct integration of the square give
$$
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{n^6}=\frac{\pi^6}{945}.
$$
